steps.body:
This lesson uses one table of bank accounts. The CHECK keeps every balance at zero or more.

```sql harness
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS accounts CASCADE;
{{code}}
```

```sql
CREATE TABLE accounts (
    id integer GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,
    owner text NOT NULL,
    balance numeric(10, 2) NOT NULL CHECK (balance >= 0)
);
-- rows: (1, 'Ana', 200.00), (2, 'Bo', 75.00), (3, 'Cy', 40.00)
```

Until now every statement committed on its own: the moment an `UPDATE` finished, its change was saved for good. psql calls this autocommit. A transfer needs two updates. If the second one never ran, money would leave one account and never arrive in the other.

`BEGIN` starts a **transaction**. The statements after it are drafts until `COMMIT` saves them all at once. Other sessions do not see the drafts, and a crash or a closed session before the commit throws all of them away, never half. So never forget the `COMMIT`.

```sql
BEGIN;
UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 10 WHERE owner = 'Cy';
UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + 10 WHERE owner = 'Ana';
COMMIT;
```

psql prints a tag for each statement: `BEGIN`, `UPDATE 1`, `UPDATE 1`, `COMMIT`. `START TRANSACTION` is the standard SQL spelling of `BEGIN` and works the same.

