steps.body:
Sometimes you want to undo only the last part of a transaction. `SAVEPOINT name` marks a spot inside it, and `ROLLBACK TO SAVEPOINT name` undoes everything after that mark while keeping what came before. The transaction stays open, so you still finish it with `COMMIT` or `ROLLBACK`.

```sql
BEGIN;
UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + 20 WHERE owner = 'Bo';
SAVEPOINT before_fee;
UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 2 WHERE owner = 'Bo';
ROLLBACK TO SAVEPOINT before_fee;  -- only the fee is undone
COMMIT;
```

Watch the tags. `SAVEPOINT` prints `SAVEPOINT`, but `ROLLBACK TO SAVEPOINT` prints plain `ROLLBACK`, even though the transaction is still going. The savepoint survives that rollback, so you can return to it again.

`RELEASE SAVEPOINT name` forgets a savepoint once you are done with it. Changes made since the mark stay in the transaction. It prints `RELEASE` and saves nothing for good: only `COMMIT` does that.

Savepoints also rescue a failed transaction. After an error, `ROLLBACK TO SAVEPOINT` clears the failure and keeps the work done before the mark, so you do not have to start over.

example.code:
BEGIN;
UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + 100 WHERE owner = 'Cy';
SAVEPOINT before_fee;
UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 5 WHERE owner = 'Cy';
ROLLBACK TO SAVEPOINT before_fee;
RELEASE SAVEPOINT before_fee;
COMMIT;
SELECT * FROM accounts ORDER BY id;

